    "message": "feat(desktop): ship the real brand mark and an opt-in desktop launcher\n\nThe desktop shell had no logo. `app-icon.png` was a 2,567-byte \"MC\" monogram\ncommitted as a real file, and `desktop_assets.py` described it as a runtime\nplaceholder for an asset that \"has not shipped\" -- so nothing rendered the\nproduct's actual mark, and there was no way to put it in a Start Menu entry\nor a Dock.\n\nThe owner supplied the designed mark. It is keyed to transparency with an\nun-blend (`C = (observed - (1-a)*bg)/a`) rather than a colour threshold: the\nsource background is a flat #050c1d and a threshold leaves a navy fringe on\nevery antialiased edge, which is exactly what shows at 16px.\n\nTwo PNG renders ship on purpose, not one file scaled at render time. A tray\ndraws at 16-24px, where the 10% margin the app icon wants shrinks the glyph\nbelow legibility, so `tray-icon.png` is the same mark cut at 2%. Palette\nquantising was tried and reverted: it folds the fully transparent background\ninto a nearest palette entry with non-zero alpha, shipping a faint box behind\nthe mark. Verified by probing the decoded alpha channel for a zero value.\n\n`--desktop` / `-Desktop` is opt-in and never runs on a default install. It\nwrites a Start Menu .lnk on Windows, a freedesktop .desktop entry on Linux,\nand a minimal .app bundle on macOS. Failure to create one warns and continues\n-- an installer's rollback story matters more than its success path, and a\nmissing icon must not cost the user the shortcut.\n\nVerification:\n\n- All four assets confirmed inside the built wheel by listing it, not by\n  reading the packaging config:\n      my_claude_code/assets/app-icon.icns   356091\n      my_claude_code/assets/app-icon.ico    115462\n      my_claude_code/assets/app-icon.png     72585\n      my_claude_code/assets/tray-icon.png    28535\n  Hatch picks them up automatically because they sit under the package, so no\n  force-include entry was needed.\n- The wheel was installed into an isolated UV_TOOL_DIR and `mcc-desktop\n  --export-icon` produced byte-exact .ico/.icns/.png output.\n- install.sh parses under `sh -n` (LF-normalised); install.ps1 parses via the\n  PowerShell AST parser with zero errors.\n- Full suite: 6 failed, 4231 passed, 26 skipped -- the six documented\n  PowerShell installer failures, identical to unmodified fork/main measured in\n  the same shell (6 failed, 4214 passed). Zero new failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty: clean.\n\nA shipped bug was caught before merge and fixed. `mcc-desktop` is a\n[project.gui-scripts] entry, so `mcc-desktop.exe` is a GUI-subsystem binary and\nPowerShell's call operator does not wait for it. The first version exported the\nicon through `Invoke-NativeCommand`, which returned in 476ms with LASTEXITCODE\n0 while the file did not yet exist, then pointed IconLocation at it -- a\nshortcut with a blank icon, reported as success. Measured:\n\n    call-operator returned after : 476 ms\n    LASTEXITCODE                 : 0\n    icon exists IMMEDIATELY after: False\n    icon exists after 6s         : True\n\nNow uses Start-Process -Wait -PassThru and verifies the file before use.\n\nDeliberately not done: the tray still reads `app_icon_bytes(\".png\")` rather\nthan the new `tray_icon_bytes()`. `desktop_tray.py` is owned by the window PR\nin flight; the wiring lands there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
